## Commands Learned
 whoami - Tells you who yoou are, usefull when swithcing users and need to make sure who you are logged in as.
 echo - output some specific text that is provided e.x in terminal type "echo test" hit enter would print test
 ls - list what's in the current folder - accidently pressed l and enter and listed folders (quick search it is an alias of ls -CF)
 cd - change directory - move folder ex. "cd Desktop" changes working directory to Desktop ("cd .." returns to the previous directory or goes one back)
 cat - shows the content of a file ex. "cat test.txt"
 pwd - prints working directiry - "Where am I?" ex. "pwd - /home/Desktop"
 find - searches for files by theur name. ex. "find -name password.txt"
 grep - searches inside for text. ex. " grep "password123""

## Combine Commands and capture output
 & - Runs command but does not wait for it to finish before you can do anything else. Command runs in the background - helpful for commands that might take a while to complete.
     or ones you want to keep running.
 && - Runs both commands, but waits for the first command to fninish first before the next. ex. " update && upgrade "
 > - Used to direct output. Take the output of a command and send it to a file. This operator will overwrite anything that exists in the file.
 >> - does the same thing but it will not overwrite just add the output to the bottom of the file.


